cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "FusionFlux: extrapolation, long-range saturation and physics constraints in confinement scaling"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Liam
    family-names: Salcedo
    alias: lsalcedo100
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-5039-8147"
repository-code: "https://github.com/lsalcedo100/FusionFlux"
abstract: >-
  A study of energy confinement time scaling on the ITPA Global H-mode
  Confinement Database (HDB5, standard analysis set STD5). Under
  cross-validation grouped by discharge a random forest improves on the
  published IPB98(y,2) scaling law by 41% in RMSLE. Holding out an entire
  tokamak instead reverses the ranking of the three blind models exactly,
  and the failure is shown to be a measurable function of extrapolation
  distance and of the training-range bound inherent to tree ensembles.
  Imposing the Connor-Taylor dimensional constraints as linear equalities on
  the exponents repairs it and is the best blind model at a size cut matched
  to ITER's, with no hyperparameter. The reversal replicates on rows the
  standard set does not contain and in a second confinement regime, and a
  locked prediction is recorded for SPARC, JT-60SA and ITER. Two results
  qualify the diagnosis. Varying only the number of predictors on a fixed
  set of woody plants shows the extrapolation failure is unconditional while
  the ranking reversal is not: the reversal requires the flexible model to
  win the interpolation split first, making it the warning sign rather than
  the hazard. And separating flexibility from boundedness with a Gaussian
  process shows the failure belongs to boundedness: a bounded kernel scores
  worse than a constant at the ITER-matched cut, while adding a linear term
  to it gives the best cross-validated score in the study together with
  intervals that reach nominal coverage out of distribution without
  recalibration. Includes a
  rank-deficiency audit of the design matrix, a from-scratch least-squares
  implementation (Cholesky, QR, SVD), a refit of the IPB98(y,2) exponents
  decomposed along the singular directions of the data, and a domain-agnostic
  module packaging the audit for other scaling-law datasets.
keywords:
  - fusion
  - tokamak
  - plasma physics
  - energy confinement time
  - scaling law
  - machine learning
  - extrapolation
  - out-of-distribution generalization
  - Gaussian processes
  - allometry
  - model selection
